### 2. Give parallel tests separate accounts

`storageState` isolates browser session data, **not** the server-side language setting. Since all 20 specs change the same account, use a disposable account per test and create that test’s storage state from it. The account-provisioning and login functions below should use your app’s test-user API.

```ts
import { test as base, expect } from '@playwright/test';

type Picker = { id: string };

export const test = base.extend<{ picker: Picker }>({
  picker: async ({}, use) => {
    const picker = await provisionPicker(); // Create a unique test account
    await use(picker);
    await deletePicker(picker.id);
  },

  storageState: async ({ picker }, use) => {
    await use(await loginAndGetStorageState(picker));
  },
});

export { expect };
```

Have the specs import `test` from this fixture module. If you can’t create isolated accounts, the fallback is to run those tests sequentially against the shared account—but that gives up parallelism.

